      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "The aim of implantation of cementless hip prostheses is vital ingrowth of bone into the structured metal surface of the implant. Since the 1960s several implants with surfaces made of cobalt-based alloys have been produced for this purpose. In the 1980s a novel hip endoprosthesis with a spongiosa-metal surface was introduced. The three-dimensional ingrowth of bone tissue into the structured surface of the implant could be demonstrated both histologically and using scanning electron microscopy (SEM). These implants made of cobalt-based alloys can also be used in endo-exo prostheses. Titanium implants with a microstructured surface have also been used and very good osseintegration of the surface was also demonstrated by histomorphology. The optimization of the surface and design of the prostheses plays an increasingly more important role in the field of revision endoprostheses."},
